== Treatment of Unsettled Capital [Sec. 37] <37>

Where a partner had died or cease to be a partner, other partners may continue to carry on the business *without any final settlement* between them and the outgoing partner/representative of the diseased.

In such case, the outgoing partner or the representative of the deceased have 2 options(unless there is a contract regarding this matter):

1. Share of profit from the day he ceased to be a partner
2. Interest at 6% p.a. _Unsettled capital is treated like a loan or advance._

In case there were a contract between partners, which gives the continuing partners an option to buy out the outgoing/diseased partners share. 

- if such option is exercised - the outgoing partner or represent can't claim the share of profit or 6% interest said above anymore.
- not exercised/failed to pay on schedule/didn't follow the agreed terms - can claim the share of profit or 6% interest said above.

=== Illustration

Net Profit Earned = ₹1,000 \
Days since he ceased to be a partner = 180 days \
Total Capital of the Firm = ₹50,000 \
Outgoing Partner's Unsettled Capital = ₹10,000 \

*Option 01 - Share of Profit in capital contribution ratio = #underline[₹98.63]*

$ "₹1000" times frac("₹10000", "₹50000") times frac("180 days", "365 days") = "₹98.63" $

*Option 02 - Interest on Capital = #underline[₹295.89]*

$ "₹10,000" times 6% times frac("180 days", "365 days") = "₹295.89" $
 
The receiver of such amount gets to choose whichever option he likes.

#note[
*Why this rule exists?* \
It prevents continuing partners from endlessly delaying the settlement of accounts while using a former partner's capital for free.
]